{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["And to Sarah he said — these are words of appeasement and consolation: 'When I first took you, it was not by coercion or by force, but according to the custom of marriage, for I gave your brother a betrothal gift of a thousand silver pieces,' because he thought her brother was indeed her brother, as she had told him. And this is not the gift of sheep and cattle that Avimelekh gave to Avraham at the end, but rather before he brought her to his house — for even with Pharaoh, who was punished more, it is written: ״ולאברם היטיב בעבורה״ before marriage, all the more so Avimelekh." "I have given a thousand silver — at the outset, as written regarding Pharaoh, and as written: ״הרבו עלי מאד מהר ומתן״. And as written: ״ומגדנות נתן לאחיה ולאמה״." "Behold, it is for you a covering of eyes for all who are with you, and for all — the thousand silver that I gave your brother at the outset is a great honor for you, and a covering of the eyes of all your household who are with you, and also for all the world, so that they will not look at you with disgrace, saying, 'This woman — Avimelekh treated her in a promiscuous manner.' For everyone will know that he took her in an honorable way and returned her against his will." "Covering of eyes — so that they will not see in you disgrace, as written: ״וכסה את עין הארץ״, and: ״ולא יוכל לראות את הארץ״. And when they gaze at a person to shame him, Scripture writes: ״לראוה בך״, ״עיני תראינה בה״, ״ותחז בציון עינינו״." "And you are vindicated — made known and properly argued, for 'I treated you honorably, and do not take it to heart except as good.' This is the core meaning according to its plain sense, for Avimelekh said all this for Sarah's honor and not to taunt or reproach her."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {3 "The five scriptural citations are given without addresses by Rashbam. The first two illustrate כסות/כסה in the sense of covering or blocking eyes/vision; the last three illustrate ראיה used in the sense of looking at someone with intent to shame or scrutinize. Their addresses (Exodus 10:5; Genesis 13:10; Psalm 100?) are not certain in each case."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:44:46.307248410Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}